    "9f71babd-79e6-4e55-9a01-65bd0dde1e4b"
   ]
  },
  "9f71babd-79e6-4e55-9a01-65bd0dde1e4b": {
   "id": "9f71babd-79e6-4e55-9a01-65bd0dde1e4b",
   "message": {
    "id": "9f71babd-79e6-4e55-9a01-65bd0dde1e4b",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1769055174.436,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "There's no correct value. It's a granularity choice, but you can make it principled:\n\n1. **Decide what a cluster is for.** If an instructor will read cluster labels, ~20–60 clusters is a readable range. 190 isn't.\n2. **Sweep and plot** `min_cluster_size` ∈ {10, 15, 20, 25} against (a) number of clusters, (b) noise fraction, (c) mean DBCV / relative validity (`clusterer.relative_validity_`).\n3. **Decouple `min_samples` from `min_cluster_size`.** By default `min_samples = min_cluster_size`, which makes bigger sizes much more conservative (more noise). Try `min_cluster_size=15, min_samples=5`. You'll usually get fewer, larger clusters without the noise blowing up.\n4. **Check stability:** rerun with 3 UMAP seeds; good clusters persist.\n\nThen pick the setting where clusters are readable and noise is tolerable (often 25–40% for forum text, which is fine. Forum posts are messy)."
     ]
    },
    "status": "finished_successfully",
    "end_turn": true,
