  "a369c46e-ba48-43ab-bb35-2aa947796fd1": {
   "id": "a369c46e-ba48-43ab-bb35-2aa947796fd1",
   "message": {
    "id": "a369c46e-ba48-43ab-bb35-2aa947796fd1",
    "author": {
     "role": "assistant",
     "name": null,
     "metadata": {}
    },
    "create_time": 1769055399.799,
    "update_time": null,
    "content": {
     "content_type": "text",
     "parts": [
      "Give the LLM representative posts, not random ones: the posts closest to each cluster's centroid (or highest `probabilities_`), plus a couple of edge members.\n\n```text\nYou are labeling clusters of student posts from an online course discussion forum.\n\nCluster {id} — {n} posts. Below are 12 representative posts (closest to the cluster center)\nand 3 posts near the cluster's edge.\n\nREPRESENTATIVE:\n1. \"...\"\n...\nEDGE:\n13. \"...\"\n\nReturn JSON:\n{\n  \"label\": \"<3-6 word topic label, specific to this course content>\",\n  \"description\": \"<one sentence: what students are discussing or asking>\",\n  \"post_type\": \"question | answer | logistics | social | mixed\",\n  \"confidence\": \"high | medium | low\",\n  \"outliers\": [<indices of posts that don't fit>]\n}\nDo not invent topics not present in the posts.\n```\n\nThen hand-check every label (41 is small enough). Mark ones where you'd change the label and report the agreement rate, since reviewers will ask how much the labels depended on the LLM."
     ]
    },
